STIR DASHBOARD

The Four Variables

Variable Name Definition
S Stability Physiological and emotional baseline. Can the system hold steady under load?
T Tools Accessible skills and strategies the client can actually deploy.
I Internalization How deeply skills have moved from external guidance into self-system.
R Reflection Capacity to observe, learn, and generate useful data from experience.

---

MEASUREMENT

Rating the Variables

S — Stability (0 – 10)

· 0 = offline / acute dysregulation
· 10 = steady under significant load

T — Tools (0 – 10)

· 0 = no usable skills in the moment
· 10 = flexible toolkit ready for use

I — Internalization (0.1 – 2.0)

· Multiplier: skill lives inside the client vs. requiring external scaffolding

R — Reflection (0 – 10)

· 0 = no reflective capacity
· 10 = high-resolution learning from every event

---

COMPUTATION

The Mastery Equations

Surface Mastery — M₁ = (S + T) · I + R
Altitude — how high the system is currently flying, including reflective gain.

Deep Capacity — M₂ = (S + T) · I − R
Depth — structural capacity after accounting for reflective load.

Operational Ranges

Score Interpretation
0 – 15 Stabilize
16 – 30 Moderate
31+ Deep Work

---

FAIL-SAFE

The Circuit Breaker

If S + T < 5 → Safe Mode

When Stability and Tools together fall below threshold, the system is not ready for exploratory or trauma-processing work. Protocol shifts to containment, regulation, and resource building only.

No deep work. No exposure. No EMDR upgrade. Stabilize first.

---

DIAGNOSTICS

Mismatch Patterns

Pattern Signature Description
Performing Wellness High S + Low I Looks stable — skills not owned
Knowledge Without Power High T + Low I Knows the tools — can't use them under load
Stagnant Processor Low R Experience happens — learning does not
System Crash S + T < 5 Circuit breaker tripped — Safe Mode only

---

PATTERN 1 OF 4 — Performing Wellness

High S · Low I

The client presents as regulated and functional. Skills exist externally but have not become self-sustaining. Progress looks good on the surface; relapse risk is high when scaffolding is removed.

PRIMARY INTERVENTIONS:

· Prioritize internalization (IFS, self-led practice)
· Reduce external prompting; test independent use
· Track I carefully across sessions
· Avoid deep work until I rises

---

PATTERN 2 OF 4 — Knowledge Without Power

High T · Low I

The client can name many tools yet cannot access them when dysregulated. Knowledge is intellectual, not embodied or automatic.

PRIMARY INTERVENTIONS:

· Move from teaching to rehearsal under mild load
· Pair tools with somatic anchors
· Practice while state is activated (titration)
· Measure by in-the-moment use, not quizzes

---

PATTERN 3 OF 4 — Stagnant Processor

Low R

Events occur and the client survives them, but little Reflection Data is generated. Loops stay the same width and altitude. Without reflective capacity, the system cannot climb.

PRIMARY INTERVENTIONS:

· Explicit R' loop entry every session
· Structured post-event journaling / Flight Log
· Therapist models reflective language
· Lower GRIT until R begins to rise

---

PATTERN 4 OF 4 — System Crash

S + T < 5

Circuit Breaker is tripped. Low stability plus low available tools means the system cannot safely process new material. Continuing exploratory work risks further destabilization.

PRIMARY INTERVENTIONS:

· Immediate Safe Mode
· Somatic regulation and resource installation only
· No trauma processing, no exposure, no EMDR
· Rebuild S and T until breaker resets (≥ 5)

